{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfs73iPxWk3yxF8HVsc1F","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed `metrics/counter.py`. `tests/test_metrics.py` passed on all 5 runs. A stress run of 4 writer threads across 50 names while another thread called `snapshot()` in a loop gave the expected total of 12000.\n\n**Cause:** `incr` read the value, yielded, then wrote it back with no lock. Two threads could read the same value, and one increment was lost. `snapshot()` and `reset()` also touched the dict while other threads were writing.\n\n**Fix:**\n- **Per-name locks:** each counter name has its own `threading.Lock`, so `incr`, `get` and `reset(name)` on different names don't wait on each other.\n- **Registry lock:** a small lock guards only the creation of those per-name locks, so two threads can't create different locks for the same name.\n- **`snapshot()` and `reset()` with no name:** these take the registry lock and then every name lock, so you get a consistent point-in-time view. They are the only operations that block increments across all names, and only while they run. `snapshot()` still returns a plain `dict` copy.\n- **No deadlock:** `incr` never waits for the registry lock while holding a name lock, so the order of lock acquisition can't produce a deadlock.\n- **Locks are kept after reset:** per-name locks stay in the registry after `reset`, which avoids races over lock lifetime. The cost is a small leak if you keep creating one-off names.\n\nThe API is unchanged."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2434,"cache_read_input_tokens":29778,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2434},"output_tokens":475,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id"